::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Passage: The station clock had stopped, but the benches were polished by years of travelers. Source note: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize movement, memory, and connection. Which research question best fits? {
=A. How does the stopped station clock and worn seating develop the idea of memory in a public space?#Correct. This question can be answered with the passage and source note. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. Who built the station clock and benches?#This asks for historical facts not provided by the literary passage. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. What kind of wood are the benches made from?#This detail does not support the Unit 4 research focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Why are train stations better than markets?#This adds an unsupported comparison. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source note: Transit spaces in literature can symbolize movement, memory, and connection. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. Transit spaces are always happy places in literature.#This adds an unsupported emotion. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Literature should include trains when discussing memory.#This changes the source idea. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Places connected to travel can represent movement, remembered experiences, and human connection.#Correct. This accurately restates the source note. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Transit spaces are only symbols when a clock appears.#This adds a condition not in the source. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
::ENG3_U04_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Passage: The station clock had stopped, but the benches were polished by years of travelers. Which literary analysis claim is strongest? {
~A. The station has a clock and benches.#This is summary only. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. The stopped clock and worn benches create a contrast between time frozen and memory preserved through use.#Correct. This is a defensible literary analysis claim. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. The station should repair its clock.#This is practical advice. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Travelers probably liked sitting on the benches.#This is unsupported speculation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}
